te.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-22\">Open Topic 22</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare an annuity’s payout and liquidity with retaining a portfolio and using scheduled withdrawals.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/index.html\">Texas Department of Insurance consumer guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-2-6","title":"Course 2 · Class 6 · Life insurance structures","html":"<h3>Course 2 · Class 6 · Life insurance structures</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-2-5\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-2\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-2-7\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-23\">Open Topic 23</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Trace policy ownership, insured, beneficiary and source of premiums for term and permanent options.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/index.html\">Texas Department of Insurance consumer guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-2-7","title":"Course 2 · Class 7 · Quantify coverage and compare policies","html":"<h3>Course 2 · Class 7 · Quantify coverage and compare policies</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-2-6\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-2\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-2-8\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-25\">Open Topic 25</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-26\">Open Topic 26</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Recalculate the death benefit gap when employer coverage ends and compare issued policy terms.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/index.html\">Texas Department of Insurance consumer guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-2-8","title":"Course 2 · Class 8 · Property, liability and business coverage","html":"<h3>Course 2 · Class 8 · Property, liability and business coverage</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-2-7\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-2\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-2-9\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-24\">Open Topic 24</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-53\">Open Topic 53</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Classify a property claim, a third-party injury and a buy-sell funding need.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/index.html\">Texas Department of Insurance consumer guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-2-9","title":"Course 2 · Class 9 · Equity compensation and deferred pay","html":"<h3>Course 2 · Class 9 · Equity compensation and deferred pay</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-2-8\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-2\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-2-10\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Annuities, social insurance and employer benefits</h4><p>An annuity can convert assets into an income stream or provide tax-deferred accumulation. Compare payout promises, liquidity, fees, inflation exposure and insurer credit. When reviewing benefits, separate employer group coverage, Social Security, Medicare and workers’ compensation by eligibility and event. Review employer plan materials for what happens at termination. Equity compensation adds employer concentration and depends on grant, vesting, exercise and sale terms. Nonqualified deferred compensation can add employer-credit risk and payment restrictions.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>A retiree considers committing $200,000 to an immediate annuity paying an illustrative $12,000 per year. The 6% payout rate includes the contract’s return-of-capital and mortality economics. Compare guaranteed income, survivor terms and lost access to principal. For a key employee with stock options or deferred compensation, model each cash-flow date and a simultaneous job loss and stock decline.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>A client plans to exercise stock options and immediately spend the reported intrinsic value. Which facts would you request first?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Identify grant type, vested shares, strike price, market value, exercise cost, sale restrictions and tax or withholding treatment. Use net realizable proceeds and stress-test employer concentration.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-49\">Open Topic 49</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-42\">Open Topic 42</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Build a grant-to-sale timeline. Model after-tax proceeds and a simultaneous job-loss and employer-share decline.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/index.html\">Texas Department of Insurance consumer guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-2-10","title":"Course 2 · Class 10 · Risk and benefits case workshop","html":"<h3>Course 2 · Class 10 · Risk and benefits case workshop</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-2-9\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-2\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Open the next course</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-19\">Open Topic 19</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-23\">Open Topic 23</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-25\">Open Topic 25</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Complete Applied case 2 and defend the order in which coverage gaps should be addressed.</p><p><a href=\"#case-2\">Open the applied case</a></p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/index.html\">Texas Department of Insurance consumer guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-1","title":"Course 3 · Class 1 · Investment vehicles and ownership","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 1 · Investment vehicles and ownership</h3><p><a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-3-2\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Choose and value investment vehicles</h4><p>Compare what the investor actually owns. Stocks represent ownership. Bonds and promissory notes represent loan claims. Cash equivalents trade expected return for liquidity. A guaranteed investment contract depends on contract terms and the issuer’s ability to pay. Real estate and alternative funds may restrict redemption or rely on estimates. Compare cost, access, taxes and the specific source of return. Distinguish issuer risk, broad market risk, purchasing-power risk, rate risk, business risk and leverage.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>A one-year bond promises $1,050 at maturity. At a 5% required yield, value is $1,000. If required yield rises to 7%, the same cash flow is worth about $981.31. A simple stock model with a $2 next dividend, 9% required return and 4% perpetually assumed growth gives $40. Small changes to the rate gap can change the estimate materially.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>What happens to that stock estimate if the required return increases to 10%? Why is the result sensitive to this assumption?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$2 ÷ (0.10 − 0.04) ≈ $33.33. The denominator is a small difference between two estimates, so small changes can materially change valuation.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-27\">Open Topic 27</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare cash equivalents, notes, stocks, funds, GICs and real estate on return source, taxes and access.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-2","title":"Course 3 · Class 2 · Risk and market cycles","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 2 · Risk and market cycles</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-3-1\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-3-3\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-28\">Open Topic 28</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-29\">Open Topic 29</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Classify inflation, leverage and business risks and stress a near-term goal under a recession scenario.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-3","title":"Course 3 · Class 3 · Calculate and interpret returns","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 3 · Calculate and interpret returns</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-3-2\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-3-4\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-30\">Open Topic 30</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare arithmetic and compounded returns and explain how a contribution changes money-weighted results.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-4","title":"Course 3 · Class 4 · Value a bond","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 4 · Value a bond</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-3-3\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-3-5\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-32\">Open Topic 32</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Discount a single cash flow at two yields and estimate the effect of a rate shock using duration.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-5","title":"Course 3 · Class 5 · Value a stock","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 5 · Value a stock</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-3-4\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-3-6\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-32\">Open Topic 32</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compute a dividend discount value at two required returns and explain sensitivity.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-6","title":"Course 3 · Class 6 · Construct an allocation","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 6 · Construct an allocation</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-3-5\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-3-7\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-31\">Open Topic 31</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Translate the client’s horizon into a mix and estimate what must trade to rebalance it.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-7","title":"Course 3 · Class 7 · Portfolio theory and pricing models","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 7 · Portfolio theory and pricing models</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-3-6\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-3-8\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Measure portfolio risk and return</h4><p>Use geometric compounding to measure growth through time. Arithmetic averages describe individual period returns. Time-weighted return reduces distortion from the timing of external contributions, while money-weighted return reflects the investor’s actual timing. Diversification works when holdings respond differently. Beta estimates market sensitivity, while volatility captures overall dispersion. CAPM estimates an expected or required return from a risk-free rate, beta and market risk premium. Use it as a model, with explicit assumptions.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>A $100 portfolio rises 25% to $125 and loses 20% to return to $100. Its cumulative return is zero. With a 3% risk-free rate, beta of 1.2 and 5% market premium, CAPM gives 3% + 1.2 × 5% = 9%. That output depends on the chosen beta and premium, and it is not a return guarantee. Before choosing a strategy, write the portfolio’s goals, benchmark, liquidity constraints and rebalancing rules.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>A 60% stock and 40% bond portfolio earns 8% and 3% respectively before rebalancing. What is the one-period portfolio return?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>0.60 × 8% + 0.40 × 3% = 6%. Use beginning weights for the stated period. A full risk estimate also needs volatility and correlation.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-33\">Open Topic 33</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compute beta-based required return, then compare portfolios with a risk-adjusted measure.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-8","title":"Course 3 · Class 8 · Investment strategies and taxes","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 8 · Investment strategies and taxes</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-3-7\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-3-9\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-34\">Open Topic 34</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-27\">Open Topic 27</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare active and passive choices and identify embedded gains and wash-sale considerations.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-9","title":"Course 3 · Class 9 · Alternatives and access to cash","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 9 · Alternatives and access to cash</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-3-8\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-3-10\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-35\">Open Topic 35</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Build a liquidity ladder with a capital call and a restricted redemption window.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-3-10","title":"Course 3 · Class 10 · Investment case workshop","html":"<h3>Course 3 · Class 10 · Investment case workshop</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-3-9\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-3\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Open the next course</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-31\">Open Topic 31</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-32\">Open Topic 32</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-33\">Open Topic 33</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Complete Applied case 3 and defend an after-tax trade sequence.</p><p><a href=\"#case-3\">Open the applied case</a></p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/\">SEC investor education</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-1","title":"Course 4 · Class 1 · Tax-year facts and authority","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 1 · Tax-year facts and authority</h3><p><a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-4-2\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-36\">Open Topic 36</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Select the tax year, filing status and source documents for a fictional household.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-2","title":"Course 4 · Class 2 · Compute taxable income and tax","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 2 · Compute taxable income and tax</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-4-1\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-4-3\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-37\">Open Topic 37</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Lay out gross income, AGI, deductions and credits, then calculate an incremental decision cost.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-3","title":"Course 4 · Class 3 · Tax accounting and timing","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 3 · Tax accounting and timing</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-4-2\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-4-4\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Tax mechanics, basis and property disposition</h4><p>Identify the tax year, taxpayer and filing status. Track recognized income and deductions through taxable income, then apply credits at the correct stage. Cash-method timing generally follows receipt and payment subject to rules. Accrual timing follows recognition criteria and can differ from cash flow. Adjusted basis is starting basis plus qualifying additions less reductions such as depreciation. When selling, compute gain from amount realized less adjusted basis before analyzing character and recapture.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>A business asset costs $80,000 and has $30,000 of depreciation adjustments. Adjusted basis is $50,000. It sells for $65,000 net. The gain is $15,000. Determine whether recapture or another classification applies before multiplying by any rate. For a potential like-kind exchange, first test whether both assets are eligible real property held for qualifying purposes. Then analyze deadlines, cash received and deferred basis with a specialist.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>If the net sale proceeds fall to $45,000, what is the result before character and limitation rules?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>A $5,000 loss, calculated as $45,000 less $50,000. Verify business use, asset class and applicable loss treatment.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-36\">Open Topic 36</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-38\">Open Topic 38</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare cash versus accrual timing with billed revenue and payments straddling year-end.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-4","title":"Course 4 · Class 4 · Compare business entities","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 4 · Compare business entities</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-4-3\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-4-5\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-38\">Open Topic 38</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Chart entity-level taxes, pass-through items, payroll and distribution questions.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-5","title":"Course 4 · Class 5 · Calculate basis and depreciation","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 5 · Calculate basis and depreciation</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-4-4\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-4-6\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-41\">Open Topic 41</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Build an asset basis roll-forward and identify applicable depreciation assumptions.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-6","title":"Course 4 · Class 6 · Disposition and like-kind exchanges","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 6 · Disposition and like-kind exchanges</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-4-5\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-4-7\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-41\">Open Topic 41</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compute gain, then flag recapture, eligible real property and exchange deadlines.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-7","title":"Course 4 · Class 7 · AMT, basis, at-risk and passive losses","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 7 · AMT, basis, at-risk and passive losses</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-4-6\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-4-8\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Entity taxation and loss limits</h4><p>A C corporation generally pays its own federal income tax. Partnerships and S corporations generally pass items through, but owner basis, payroll and distributions can differ. Determine whether an owner can use a reported loss before promising a current deduction. Test basis, amount at risk and passive restrictions in the applicable order, and check any further limitation. AMT is a parallel calculation with specified adjustments and preferences. Model it separately when facts such as incentive stock option exercise warrant review.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>Assume a pass-through owner is allocated a $30,000 loss. Their applicable basis supports $24,000, their amount at risk supports $18,000, and only $5,000 is currently usable after the stated passive limitation. Under the exercise assumptions, $5,000 is usable now. Document amounts separately at each limitation because later release depends on the relevant rule. Owner draws and taxable income can differ.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>If basis is $24,000, amount at risk is $18,000 and $12,000 survives the passive limitation, what is currently usable under the stated assumptions?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$12,000, before any additional rule. Passing the basis test alone does not establish current deductibility.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-42\">Open Topic 42</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-38\">Open Topic 38</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Apply three sequential loss limits and describe why an option exercise can warrant an AMT model.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-8","title":"Course 4 · Class 8 · Trust and estate income tax","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 8 · Trust and estate income tax</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-4-7\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-4-9\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-39\">Open Topic 39</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-59\">Open Topic 59</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Trace a simplified distribution from a nongrantor trust and identify the role of distributable net income.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-9","title":"Course 4 · Class 9 · Giving, divorce and death","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 9 · Giving, divorce and death</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-4-8\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-4-10\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Charitable giving, death and divorce</h4><p>A charitable deduction depends on the recipient, asset, holding period, limits, documentation and the taxpayer’s circumstances. Compare selling an appreciated asset and giving cash with transferring the asset directly. After death, check return filing, possible surviving-spouse treatment, beneficiary distributions and basis. Divorce changes filing-status analysis and can affect property transfers, dependents and retirement division. Verify effective dates and governing documents.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>Stock worth $40,000 has $12,000 basis. At an illustrative 15% gain rate, selling before donating would create $4,200 of modeled tax on the $28,000 gain. A qualifying direct gift can avoid that donor realization. Check whether deductions are usable and how each path affects the amount delivered to charity. For a recently divorced client, get the decree and examine which spouse can claim applicable benefits before preparing a comparison.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>If the asset’s basis is $30,000 instead, how much tax would the same sale generate in the simplified model?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>15% of $10,000, or $1,500. A high-basis asset has less embedded gain, so the relative benefit of donating it directly changes.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-43\">Open Topic 43</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-42\">Open Topic 42</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-63\">Open Topic 63</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare two noncash gifts and identify how a change in marital status could change return inputs.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-4-10","title":"Course 4 · Class 10 · Tax case workshop","html":"<h3>Course 4 · Class 10 · Tax case workshop</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-4-9\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-4\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Open the next course</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-40\">Open Topic 40</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-41\">Open Topic 41</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-43\">Open Topic 43</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Complete Applied case 4 and compare an alternative with a different tax year or basis assumption.</p><p><a href=\"#case-4\">Open the applied case</a></p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p544\">IRS property dispositions</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-1","title":"Course 5 · Class 1 · Estimate retirement needs","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 1 · Estimate retirement needs</h3><p><a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-5-2\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-44\">Open Topic 44</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Build a today-dollar spending forecast and isolate the income the portfolio must replace.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-2","title":"Course 5 · Class 2 · Social Security and Medicare","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 2 · Social Security and Medicare</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-5-1\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-5-3\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-45\">Open Topic 45</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare two claiming scenarios and model survivor income. List Medicare enrollment facts to verify.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-3","title":"Course 5 · Class 3 · Caregiving and special-needs planning","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 3 · Caregiving and special-needs planning</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-5-2\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-5-4\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-46\">Open Topic 46</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Estimate the household’s care budget and identify benefits questions for a specialist.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-4","title":"Course 5 · Class 4 · Retirement plan types","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 4 · Retirement plan types</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-5-3\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-5-5\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-47\">Open Topic 47</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare a formula-based benefit with an individual account, then match plans to worker situations.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-5","title":"Course 5 · Class 5 · ERISA, eligibility and vesting","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 5 · ERISA, eligibility and vesting</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-5-4\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-5-6\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Employer plans, ERISA and business design</h4><p>In a defined contribution plan, investment decisions and contributions drive an account balance. A defined benefit plan promises a formula benefit. For employer-sponsored plans, determine whether ERISA applies and how plan fiduciary, disclosure, eligibility and vesting rules affect the decision. Compare SEP, SIMPLE, 401(k) and defined benefit options using owner goals, eligible employees, variable cash flow and administration. Model the employee cost and document fees before focusing on the owner’s contribution.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>A firm with uneven profit wants flexibility in years with lower revenue. Compare how plan contribution commitments, employee coverage and administrative costs change under several options. In a stylized 401(k) match of 50% on the first 6% of $100,000 pay, a $6,000 employee contribution earns $3,000 before vesting or other constraints.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>What match is earned on a $4,000 contribution in this stylized plan? What should the planner verify before recommending a choice?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$2,000. Verify eligibility, vesting, pay definition, timing, fees and whether the employer matches on a payroll or annual basis.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-48\">Open Topic 48</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Calculate an employer match and identify which plan documents govern eligibility and vesting.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-6","title":"Course 5 · Class 6 · Nonqualified and executive benefits","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 6 · Nonqualified and executive benefits</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-5-5\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-5-7\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-49\">Open Topic 49</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Map deferral, vesting and payment risk to a timeline for a key employee.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-7","title":"Course 5 · Class 7 · Choose a business retirement plan","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 7 · Choose a business retirement plan</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-5-6\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-5-8\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-50\">Open Topic 50</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-53\">Open Topic 53</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare owner goals, payroll cost, eligibility and contribution flexibility.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-8","title":"Course 5 · Class 8 · Rollovers and distribution tax","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 8 · Rollovers and distribution tax</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-5-7\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-5-9\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Distribution choices, claiming and portfolio income</h4><p>First identify account type, money source, age, beneficiary and plan terms. Compare direct rollovers, taxable distributions and conversions. Review required distributions and whether a proposed payment is eligible to move. Check Social Security claiming amounts, survivor benefits and the need for cash before comparing ages. Treat Medicare enrollment separately. In retirement, model the draw after dependable income and stress-test a portfolio decline early in the withdrawal period.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>A client needs $75,000 a year including taxes. Reliable income provides $42,000 and investable assets are $825,000. The initial $33,000 draw is 4%. After a 20% decline, assets are $660,000 and the same draw is 5%. Compare reducing discretionary spending, keeping a cash buffer and using other eligible income. For each account, show expected tax and cash actually received.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>How much annual spending reduction returns the draw to 4% of $660,000, with reliable income unchanged?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$6,600. Four percent of $660,000 is $26,400, compared with the $33,000 gap. This is a one-year estimate rather than a complete retirement projection.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-51\">Open Topic 51</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Classify a proposed payment as eligible rollover, conversion or current distribution before modeling tax.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-9","title":"Course 5 · Class 9 · Invest and withdraw in retirement","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 9 · Invest and withdraw in retirement</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-5-8\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-5-10\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-52\">Open Topic 52</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Build a spending floor and stress-test an early portfolio decline and an unexpected expense.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-5-10","title":"Course 5 · Class 10 · Retirement case workshop","html":"<h3>Course 5 · Class 10 · Retirement case workshop</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-5-9\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-5\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#course-6\">Open the next course</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-44\">Open Topic 44</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-51\">Open Topic 51</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-52\">Open Topic 52</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Complete Applied case 5 and compare spending flexibility with holding a cash reserve.</p><p><a href=\"#case-5\">Open the applied case</a></p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/rollovers-of-retirement-plan-and-ira-distributions\">IRS rollover guidance</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-6-1","title":"Course 6 · Class 1 · Inventory title and beneficiaries","html":"<h3>Course 6 · Class 1 · Inventory title and beneficiaries</h3><p><a href=\"#course-6\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-6-2\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-54\">Open Topic 54</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Trace the legal owner and likely recipient for six assets.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estate-and-gift-taxes\">IRS estate and gift tax resources</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-6-2","title":"Course 6 · Class 2 · Transfers, wills and probate","html":"<h3>Course 6 · Class 2 · Transfers, wills and probate</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-6-1\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-6\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-6-3\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Titling, probate and incapacity</h4><p>For every asset, identify title, beneficiary designation, controlling document and when money can be accessed. A will can direct probate assets and nominate guardians. Financial and health authority during life usually require different instruments. Trust terms apply to assets actually placed in or directed to the trust. Probate treatment, estate tax inclusion and control during life are separate questions.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>Assume a cash account has a valid payable-on-death beneficiary, a home is individually titled and a brokerage account belongs to a funded revocable trust. Trace each asset separately. The cash account follows its designation, the home may need probate or another state-law process, and the trustee administers the trust account. Check state law, the actual forms and liquidity before presenting a plan.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>A client signs a trust but never changes title or beneficiary forms. What would you verify before telling them assets are controlled by the trust?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Verify transfers, beneficiary and survivorship terms, any applicable will provision and state law. Signing the trust does not by itself fund it with every asset.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-55\">Open Topic 55</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-56\">Open Topic 56</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Identify which instrument applies to each asset and where counsel must verify local law.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estate-and-gift-taxes\">IRS estate and gift tax resources</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-6-3","title":"Course 6 · Class 3 · Planning for incapacity","html":"<h3>Course 6 · Class 3 · Planning for incapacity</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-6-2\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-6\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-6-4\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-56\">Open Topic 56</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Map medical and financial decisions to the expected decision-maker and legal authority.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estate-and-gift-taxes\">IRS estate and gift tax resources</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-6-4","title":"Course 6 · Class 4 · Gifts, estate tax and GST","html":"<h3>Course 6 · Class 4 · Gifts, estate tax and GST</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-6-3\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-6\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-6-5\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-57\">Open Topic 57</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Build a transfer ledger and flag year-specific exclusions, filings and elections to verify.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estate-and-gift-taxes\">IRS estate and gift tax resources</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-6-5","title":"Course 6 · Class 5 · Estate liquidity","html":"<h3>Course 6 · Class 5 · Estate liquidity</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-6-4\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-6\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-6-6\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-58\">Open Topic 58</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Estimate immediate cash needs and test two options for an illiquid estate.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estate-and-gift-taxes\">IRS estate and gift tax resources</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-6-6","title":"Course 6 · Class 6 · Trust designs and income tax","html":"<h3>Course 6 · Class 6 · Trust designs and income tax</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-6-5\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-6\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-6-7\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the lesson below and the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the example below. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Gifting, trusts and estate liquidity</h4><p>Compare ownership during life, basis, control, beneficiary needs and when cash is available. Lifetime gifts and inherited transfers can have different basis treatment. In trust planning identify grantor, trustee, beneficiary and the powers that control tax results. A grantor trust and a separate taxpayer present different income-tax questions. Model large estate bills separately from the amount of property passing to beneficiaries.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>An estate expects $120,000 of near-term expenses. It has $40,000 of accessible cash and a $300,000 policy payable to a child. The initial estate cash shortfall is $80,000. Compare funding routes and counsel’s advice. For a lifetime gift, ask how much appreciation the recipient may inherit through basis rules before advising solely on the gift’s stated market value.</p><h4>Practice variation</h4><p>If the executor verifies another $55,000 account is accessible before the bills are due, what is the new liquidity gap?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$25,000. The life policy proceeds still go to the named child under the case assumptions.</p></details><h4>Related topic lessons</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-59\">Open Topic 59</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-39\">Open Topic 39</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare revocable, marital and special-needs trusts by control, beneficiary and tax question.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estate-and-gift-taxes\">IRS estate and gift tax resources</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-6-7","title":"Course 6 · Class 7 · Marital transfers and postmortem planning","html":"<h3>Course 6 · Class 7 · Marital transfers and postmortem planning</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-6-6\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-6\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-6-8\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-60\">Open Topic 60</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-62\">Open Topic 62</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Create a timeline for elections and discuss why distributions should be reviewed before a possible disclaimer.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estate-and-gift-taxes\">IRS estate and gift tax resources</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-6-8","title":"Course 6 · Class 8 · Family business succession","html":"<h3>Course 6 · Class 8 · Family business succession</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-6-7\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-6\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-6-9\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already know.</li><li>35 minutes · Learn the concepts using the linked topic lessons. Answer each knowledge check before revealing the answer.</li><li>25 minutes · Work through the linked topic questions. Write down the rule, inputs and interpretation.</li><li>10 minutes · Break.</li><li>30 minutes · Complete the class assignment. Explain what changes if an assumption is wrong.</li><li>10 minutes · Explain your conclusion aloud, log anything you missed and mark the class complete.</li></ol><h4>Teaching and worked practice</h4><ul><li><a href=\"#topic-61\">Open Topic 61</a></li><li><a href=\"#topic-53\">Open Topic 53</a></li></ul><h4>Class assignment</h4><p>Compare sale and gift structures on liquidity, control and fairness.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/estate-and-gift-taxes\">IRS estate and gift tax resources</a></p>"},{"id":"cfp-class-6-9","title":"Course 6 · Class 9 · Divorce, unmarried partners and special needs","html":"<h3>Course 6 · Class 9 · Divorce, unmarried partners and special needs</h3><p><a href=\"#cfp-class-6-8\">Previous class</a> · <a href=\"#course-6\">Course syllabus</a> · <a href=\"#cfp-class-6-10\">Next class</a></p><h4>Two-hour class</h4><ol><li>10 minutes · Recall what you learned in the previous class or identify what you already kno